 {"id":"codex-remote-app-workshop","title":"Plus-Powered App Workshop","category":"Development & Tools","icon":"💻","accent":"#85e6cf","status":"next","priority":"high","favorite":true,"summary":"Set up Codex on the laptop and guide coding from the iPhone, then build, test, version and publish our apps without extra monthly fees.","vision":"Turn app ideas into working, phone-friendly projects through a reliable repeatable workflow using ChatGPT Plus, Codex, GitHub, and included or free hosting—not fragile file handoffs or another paid subscription.","features":["Verify Codex desktop setup and iPhone remote control using the same ChatGPT Plus account","Keep each app's source code and history in GitHub, with a safe rollback path","Test ChatGPT Sites if available and suitable; compare free GitHub Pages or Cloudflare Pages before choosing hosting","Start with CueLab: complete working scenarios, test on a real phone, and publish a stable link","Move Gift Exchange, Card Scout, Idea Atlas and Concert Vault gradually, preserving accounts, data, and existing working features","Use repeatable checklists for requests, coding, testing, deployment and iPhone verification"],"nextStep":"On the laptop, sign into Codex using the same Apple-linked ChatGPT Plus account and verify that an iPhone can start or monitor a remote coding task. Then make CueLab the first end-to-end test.","tags":["ChatGPT Plus","Codex","iPhone","GitHub","App development","Free hosting"],"notes":"Added November 11, 2026. This is an app-development workflow project rather than a new standalone app. Existing Idea Atlas ideas and locally saved edits must stay intact. Do not assume remote control or ChatGPT Sites is available until tested. Avoid Vercel headaches; use existing GitHub Pages or another free/included option. No paid APIs, paid deployments, or additional recurring subscriptions."},
 {"id":"retroquest","title":"RetroQuest: Retro Game Companion","category":"Retro Gaming & Guides","icon":"🎮","accent":"#89cfff","status":"inbox","priority":"high","favorite":true,"summary":"A spoiler-aware iPhone companion for retro games: step-by-step walkthroughs, interactive maps, graduated hints, secrets and cheat codes.","vision":"A spoiler-aware personal gaming companion and reference hub for the user's own retro games across multiple classic platforms. Launch by developing complete Sega Genesis guides first, then expand to NES, SNES, Game Boy, PlayStation and other classic systems. Designed for a player using original cartridges on a separate console with an iPhone nearby.","features":["Support a multi-platform retro game catalog with console- and version-specific guides; develop Sega Genesis content first","Two switchable guide experiences: guided chapter-by-chapter progress with spoiler-safe hints, and a complete searchable reference library for walkthroughs, maps, cheats and strategies","Hybrid guide library: original, verified, fully interactive guides for selected games, plus a searchable directory linking to trusted external walkthroughs for broader coverage","Initial game collection: usable starter guides for Power Monger, Phantasy Star III: Generations of Doom, Phantasy Star II and King's Bounty, with expanded original guides over time; use the Sega Genesis version of King's Bounty","Both progress-navigation methods: manual saved checkpoints and checklists, plus text description lookup to locate a chapter, area or objective without paid AI","Step-by-step walkthroughs with checkable objectives and per-game progress","Two interactive map styles: switch between fully revealed zoomable reference maps and discovery maps that hide unexplored regions and secrets until the player reveals them","Dual hint options for every supported obstacle: reveal three progressive hints one at a time or jump directly to the full solution, with spoiler warnings","Organized cheats and secrets catalog per game: searchable categories, verified activation steps, platform/version compatibility, save-risk warnings, favorite codes and tried-code tracking","Game-specific strategy references such as RPG spells, equipment and enemies or strategy-game units and terrain","Simple personal game tracker: mark titles as Owned, Playing, Completed or Wishlist and open their guides; omit condition, purchase prices, dates and box/manual details from the initial release","Optional Game Companion Mode: manually select current location/objective for contextual, spoiler-safe help","Selective offline game packs: choose individual supported games to store original walkthroughs, maps, hints, cheats and progress within the iPhone-friendly app; no manual file opening, with online links to external guides remaining online-only","Free-tier automatic cloud sync of collection, progress, checkpoints, discovery-map state and notes between iPhone and laptop, including offline changes and safe conflict resolution","Google sign-in as the sole launch authentication method for free-tier cross-device cloud sync; keep offline progress safe while signed out and verify provider limits before implementation","Classic retro arcade presentation: pixel-inspired but legible fonts, nostalgic console colors, arcade-like navigation, accessible contrast and phone-friendly walkthrough/map readability","New-game onboarding: both a quick, platform-specific controller reference and an optional interactive beginner tutorial with checkable objectives, skippable lessons and remembered progress","Illustrated walkthroughs with relevant properly licensed or original screenshots, annotated diagrams and interactive maps; add optional external video demonstration links, preserving a complete non-video path","Game-specific synced bookmarks and private personal notes: save walkthrough sections, map locations and objectives; maintain a per-game notebook for discoveries, reminders and strategies, usable offline","Personal-only gaming companion for the owner, prioritizing the owner's Genesis play sessions and other chosen retro games; no public user profiles, community submissions or public content library features","Text-and-visuals-only interface for walkthroughs, steps, hints and maps; no read-aloud, voice commands, microphone access or speech integration at launch","Game-specific searchable reference charts plus manually updated interactive trackers for equipment, spells, units, bosses, collectibles and other relevant milestones; offline and cloud-synced per game","Home dashboard combining a prominent Continue Playing/resume-last-checkpoint area with a searchable visual personal game library, collection filters, favorites and map shortcuts","Single active playthrough per game: keep one set of walkthrough checkpoints, map discoveries and item trackers, with an explicit confirmation before restarting; no multiple save slots or branching-path diagrams","Automatic background updates for individually saved offline guide packs when online, using stable section IDs and preserving checkpoints, notes, bookmarks, favorites and map discoveries; recover safely if download fails","Original manual links plus searchable in-app quick references: link to official/lawfully available instruction manuals, while providing verified original summaries of controls, menus and game rules; do not host unauthorized manual copies","Manual game entry for personal library: type any title and choose its platform, set a simple collection status, save and sync it without requiring catalog search or an existing RetroQuest guide","For games lacking native walkthroughs, show verified external guide/manual links where available and add a separate synced Guide Wishlist flag to prioritize future original interactive guides","In-app guide announcements: a dismissible What's New area and New Guide indicators for newly published original guides, including Guide Wishlist titles; no push notifications","Free-tier recoverable automatic backup snapshots of personal RetroQuest data when technically feasible, plus portable manual JSON export and safe restore with verification and confirmation","Simple game completion: mark a game Completed and optionally enter the date; no RetroQuest achievement badges, trophies, experience points or gamified challenges","Owned-version-only guides: manually enter the game and console/edition actually owned, and show walkthroughs, controls, maps and cheat compatibility for that version without an unnecessary version selector","Build in tested stages: first a working manual game library, functional Power Monger companion walkthrough and durable progress on iPhone/laptop, then enrich the four starter guides and add maps, offline packs, free-tier cloud sync and refinements, verifying every stage"],"nextStep":"Follow the permanent RetroQuest Sparky handoff in idea-atlas/briefs/retroquest-sparky-handoff.md. Build the first functional local-first iPhone/laptop milestone with manually entered owned-console games and one verified Power Monger beginner guide; test saved progress before expanding to other guides, maps, offline packs and cloud sync.","tags":["Retro gaming","Sega Genesis","Walkthroughs","Maps","Hints","Cheat codes","iPhone","Free"],"notes":"Idea brainstormed November 15, 2026. User owns a Genesis-compatible console gifted by Jay that plays original cartridges. Recently acquired Power Monger (new to user) and Phantasy Star III: Generations of Doom. Also enjoys Master of Monsters, the Phantasy Star series and Sword of Vermilion. Start with Power Monger as a learning-from-scratch test case. RetroQuest is a companion, not an emulator. Create original guide text/maps, use licensed material or link to external resources rather than copying copyrighted walkthroughs. Preserve all existing Idea Atlas entries and personal data. Requirements still to be refined through a one-question-at-a-time interview. Interview decision 1 (November 15, 2026): B — support all retro gaming platforms in principle, prioritizing Sega Genesis as the first system for guide development. Later platforms include NES, SNES, Game Boy, PlayStation and others. Do not promise all platform guides at launch. Full design interview decisions will be kept in idea-atlas/briefs/retroquest-interview.md. Interview decision 2 (November 15, 2026): C — BOTH guide experiences. Offer a guided, spoiler-aware, objective-tracking companion AND a searchable full-reference library; players can switch freely between them. Interview decision 3 (November 15, 2026): C — hybrid guide library. Develop comprehensive original interactive guides for a select set of games, starting with Power Monger for Genesis, while linking to reputable outside walkthroughs to provide wider coverage. Attribute source links and do not reproduce copyrighted guides without permission. Interview decision 4 (November 15, 2026): C — offer both fully revealed zoomable reference maps and discovery maps, allowing players to switch between them. Discovery maps conceal unexplored sections and secrets, with progress revealed as the player explores/marks locations. Interview decision 5 (November 15, 2026): C — both methods. Players may manually select chapter/area/objective and mark completed steps, and also type a description of their location or last event to search for the corresponding walkthrough point. Preserve progress per game and work without a paid AI API; use deterministic local search initially. Interview decision 6 (November 15, 2026): B — release a small game starter collection, with Power Monger, Phantasy Star III: Generations of Doom, Phantasy Star II and King's Bounty specifically requested. Expand guides incrementally; confirmed user meant King's Bounty (Genesis), not the King's Quest series. First priority is a Genesis-oriented collection, not a massive directory. Interview decision 7 (November 15, 2026): C — both hints and direct answers. Players can choose for each game obstacle whether to reveal a three-step hint ladder or go directly to the solution; spoiler warnings/controls should protect discoveries. Interview decision 8 (November 15, 2026): A — simple collection tracker. Record only basic game ownership/playing/completion/wishlist status with quick guide access; no detailed pricing, condition or box/manual inventory in first release. Interview decision 9 (November 15, 2026): B — save selected games for offline use. Cache app-owned guides, maps, hints and cheats per game directly inside the app; preserve progress offline without requiring separate files. Do not copy third-party copyrighted walkthroughs for offline use without permission. Free hosting and no required paid APIs remain mandatory. Interview decision 10 (November 15, 2026): C — automatic cloud syncing of collection, guide and map progress, checkpoints and notes between phone and laptop, with changes made offline syncing on reconnection. Use a reliable free-tier solution, avoid paid APIs or additional subscriptions, protect against conflicts and preserve local backups. Account/sign-in choice is the next interview question. Interview decision 11 (November 15, 2026): A — Google sign-in only at launch, using the user's existing Google account. Do not require a separate password or email magic link. Verify free authentication-provider limits and iPhone compatibility; preserve unsynced local data. Interview question 12 will choose the visual theme. Interview decision 12 (November 15, 2026): A — classic retro arcade visual style, with pixel-inspired fonts, nostalgic console palette, arcade-style menus and retro atmosphere. Preserve legibility, accessibility and responsiveness. No switchable modern theme required for the initial release. Interview decision 13 (November 15, 2026): C — include BOTH an easy-to-access controller quick-reference guide and an optional interactive, step-by-step beginner tutorial, with saved lesson progress and skip controls. Verify game-specific controller commands before presenting them as factual. Interview decision 14 (November 15, 2026): C — guides should contain useful screenshots or appropriately licensed original illustrations, plus optional video links to relevant gameplay demonstrations. Videos must never be required to finish a guide. Observe image/video rights and offline licensing. Interview decision 15 (November 15, 2026): C — offer BOTH bookmarks and a private game notebook. Bookmarks can point to guides, individual steps and map locations; user-authored personal notes record discoveries, clues and reminders. Sync both across devices with offline support and conflict-safe updates. Interview decision 16 (November 15, 2026): C — organized cheat and secret reference with favorites. Group codes by type, include instructions, verified compatibility and warnings; players may favorite each cheat and record whether tried, syncing this state between devices. Interview decision 17 (November 15, 2026): A — PERSONAL gaming companion, for the user's own play sessions, not a public guide library or community contribution service. Preserve private personal notes, bookmarks and progress with Google sign-in; avoid assuming publicly hosted static guide assets are private. Interview decision 18 (November 15, 2026): A — TEXT AND VISUALS ONLY. Do not implement voice guidance, speech playback, voice commands or microphone permissions at launch; ensure high-quality readable text and visual guidance. Interview decision 19 (November 15, 2026): B — searchable game-specific reference charts plus interactive trackers. Track collected equipment, learned spells, defeated bosses, game-specific milestones and similar items manually. Sync progress while offline-friendly; advanced strategy calculators and planners are not part of the initial requirement. Interview decision 20 (November 15, 2026): C — BOTH home screen approaches. Feature an easy Continue Playing area for the current game/last checkpoint above a visual personal game library with search, owned/playing/completed/wishlist filters and quick access to favorites and maps. Interview decision 21 (November 15, 2026): A — only one active playthrough per game. Do not build multiple save slots or story-branch diagrams. Restarting a run should require confirmation and should not accidentally erase general bookmarks, notebook entries or collection status. Interview decision 22 (November 15, 2026): A — automatically update offline game guides and maps when an internet connection returns, never resetting saved gameplay progress, discovered areas, personal notes or bookmarks. Use safe update handling and preserve cached working guides if an update is interrupted. Interview decision 23 (November 15, 2026): C — show links to original game manuals where authorized or legally available, plus searchable, independently written and verified quick references covering menus, controls, mechanics and rules. Respect copyright; do not distribute unlicensed manual scans. Interview decision 24 (November 15, 2026): B — add games MANUALLY, entering title and system even when there is no corresponding catalog or guide. Don't require a search-based add-game flow; any future guide matching should preserve manually entered data and use a user-controlled link. Interview decision 25 (November 15, 2026): C — external guide links PLUS a Guide Wishlist for games without native RetroQuest guides. Offer reputable external walkthrough/manual links when found, and let the user flag titles to prioritize for later original interactive guides. This guide wishlist is separate from the Owned/Playing/Completed/Wishlist collection status; do not imply instant automatic guide generation. Interview decision 26 (November 15, 2026): B — in-app announcements only for newly available original guides. Show a What's New area and New Guide badges (especially for games on the Guide Wishlist), with seen/dismissed tracking; no iPhone push alerts or notification permissions. Interview decision 27 (November 15, 2026): C — automatic recoverable snapshots plus manual export and restore, for collection, guide progress, notes, bookmarks, map discoveries, favorites and wishlist. Implement only with a verified free-tier storage solution; make backup restore safe, explicit, and resistant to overwriting fresher data. Document storage/retention limits. Interview decision 28 (November 15, 2026): A — SIMPLE game completion only, with an optional date. Do not build a RetroQuest achievements/badges/XP system. Existing game-specific item/boss progress trackers remain practical checklists, not gamified achievements. Interview decision 29 (November 15, 2026): A — track only the version of a game actually owned/played. Each manual library entry has its own platform and guide context; do not build a platform-switching guide UI or duplicate editions unrequested. Ensure maps and codes match the user's exact edition. Interview decision 30 (November 15, 2026): B — BUILD AND TEST IN STAGES. Implement a real, functional personal game collection and one working spoiler-aware Power Monger guide first; validate on iPhone and laptop, then expand guides, maps, offline support and free-tier Google-auth sync in phases. Permanent build handoff: idea-atlas/briefs/retroquest-sparky-handoff.md."}
